1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,435
Part 2: the cheese app on GKE,
as Pulumi code.

2
00:00:03,755 --> 00:00:08,150
The course link lands on Deployment
with Scaling using Kubernetes;

3
00:00:08,150 --> 00:00:11,479
the cluster steps continue
under the next heading,

4
00:00:11,479 --> 00:00:13,077
Create & Deploy Cluster.

5
00:00:13,817 --> 00:00:17,124
Here is the plan,
piece by piece from lecture 20.

6
00:00:17,444 --> 00:00:20,302
Each long-running container
becomes a Deployment.

7
00:00:20,622 --> 00:00:22,913
The one-shot loader becomes a Job:

8
00:00:22,913 --> 00:00:27,833
it retries a failed run a few times,
and once it succeeds, it stays done.

9
00:00:28,367 --> 00:00:33,039
Container names on a Docker network
become Services with DNS names.

10
00:00:33,359 --> 00:00:37,613
The folders on the VM disk
become two persistent volume claims,

11
00:00:37,613 --> 00:00:39,503
each backed by a cloud disk.

12
00:00:39,750 --> 00:00:44,358
The nginx container becomes
an Ingress controller with an Ingress rule.

13
00:00:44,678 --> 00:00:49,158
The JSON key becomes Workload Identity,
with no key file at all.

14
00:00:49,478 --> 00:00:52,379
One machine becomes
a pool of one or two nodes.

15
00:00:52,617 --> 00:00:54,537
Around it, a private network.

16
00:00:54,857 --> 00:00:57,125
The nodes get no public addresses;

17
00:00:57,125 --> 00:01:02,729
a Cloud NAT gateway lets them reach out,
for example to pull Chroma from Docker Hub.

18
00:01:03,049 --> 00:01:06,846
The app sits in one namespace,
behind one load balancer.

19
00:01:08,450 --> 00:01:13,074
The program in deploy_k8s says the same
in four calls, one per file:

20
00:01:13,074 --> 00:01:17,154
the network, the cluster,
the containers, the load balancer.

21
00:01:17,474 --> 00:01:21,463
Line 17 keeps the HTTPS
variant switched off.

22
00:01:22,200 --> 00:01:26,272
While it is up, it costs money:
the fee of a regional cluster,

23
00:01:26,272 --> 00:01:30,541
one or two nodes, a load balancer,
one NAT gateway and two disks.

24
00:01:30,861 --> 00:01:34,172
By our estimate,
roughly five to eight dollars a day:

25
00:01:34,172 --> 00:01:37,795
check the pricing calculator,
and destroy it the same day.

26
00:01:40,133 --> 00:01:43,586
This section enables the five APIs again;

27
00:01:43,586 --> 00:01:48,218
turn on the Vertex AI API too,
which the API Pods call.

28
00:01:48,966 --> 00:01:52,315
You also need the two service accounts
from lecture 20.

29
00:01:52,635 --> 00:01:55,622
The deployment account
does what the toolbox does,

30
00:01:55,622 --> 00:01:58,011
and here it is also the nodes' identity.

31
00:01:58,331 --> 00:02:03,196
The gcp-service account is for the API Pods,
with no key file.

32
00:02:03,933 --> 00:02:06,408
Two one-time steps the README leaves out.

33
00:02:06,728 --> 00:02:12,049
The deployment account very likely needs
Service Account Admin on gcp-service;

34
00:02:12,049 --> 00:02:13,618
we could not test this.

35
00:02:13,938 --> 00:02:16,384
And create the registry repository,

36
00:02:16,384 --> 00:02:19,528
cheese-app-repository,
which no code creates.

37
00:02:20,266 --> 00:02:23,518
The deployment key goes
in the secrets folder,

38
00:02:23,518 --> 00:02:26,346
beside the repository, as in lecture 20.

39
00:02:27,083 --> 00:02:30,559
Then the toolbox steps from part 1,
the same commands;

40
00:02:30,559 --> 00:02:34,806
if the toolbox is still running,
the script simply attaches to it.

41
00:02:35,050 --> 00:02:39,689
Next, the images stack,
only if you have not built it before:

42
00:02:39,689 --> 00:02:41,691
change into deploy_images,

43
00:02:41,691 --> 00:02:45,695
pulumi stack init dev,
and set gcp:project to yours.

44
00:02:46,015 --> 00:02:48,975
Ours says PULUMI_ACCESS_TOKEN must be set:

45
00:02:48,975 --> 00:02:53,695
our bucket login failed,
so Pulumi falls back to its cloud service.

46
00:02:53,933 --> 00:02:57,324
To show the rest,
we point Pulumi at a local folder,

47
00:02:57,324 --> 00:02:59,736
a recording workaround: don't run it.

48
00:03:00,056 --> 00:03:02,552
Now the stack is created, as yours is.

49
00:03:02,800 --> 00:03:05,909
Then pulumi preview --stack dev:
three image builds,

50
00:03:05,909 --> 00:03:09,435
skipped in a preview,
and three tags stamped with the time.

51
00:03:09,755 --> 00:03:13,168
+ 4 to create:
three images and the stack itself.

52
00:03:13,416 --> 00:03:15,057
Those tag outputs matter:

53
00:03:15,057 --> 00:03:18,446
deploy_k8s looks them up
through a stack reference,

54
00:03:18,446 --> 00:03:20,307
to know which images to run.

55
00:03:20,550 --> 00:03:25,606
Last, pulumi up --stack dev -y:
it builds and pushes three images.

56
00:03:25,926 --> 00:03:30,615
Skip it if you did it in lecture 20,
with one condition we meet next;

57
00:03:30,615 --> 00:03:32,518
otherwise, pause and run it.

58
00:03:33,767 --> 00:03:35,730
Now Create & Deploy Cluster.

59
00:03:36,050 --> 00:03:37,960
From the deployment folder,

60
00:03:37,960 --> 00:03:41,762
change into deploy_k8s
and run pulumi stack init dev:

61
00:03:41,762 --> 00:03:43,268
the same token error,

62
00:03:43,268 --> 00:03:46,524
and on the local backend,
Created stack 'dev'.

63
00:03:47,167 --> 00:03:49,880
Then three config keys: your project,

64
00:03:49,880 --> 00:03:54,207
and two service account emails,
deployment and gcp-service.

65
00:03:54,527 --> 00:03:58,282
Replace ac215-project
with yours in all three.

66
00:03:59,784 --> 00:04:04,179
Line 9 takes the deployment email:
the nodes run as that account.

67
00:04:04,499 --> 00:04:10,109
Line 10 takes the gcp-service email,
which the API Pods will act as.

68
00:04:10,350 --> 00:04:13,788
An extra,
pulumi config, lists your three keys

69
00:04:13,788 --> 00:04:17,600
and two leftovers from the committed file,
setupSSL

70
00:04:17,600 --> 00:04:21,038
and an SSH user,
which the program never uses.

71
00:04:21,358 --> 00:04:25,177
A typo in a key name fails quietly later,
so check this list.

72
00:04:25,417 --> 00:04:28,745
Then Preview:
pulumi preview --stack dev --refresh.

73
00:04:29,065 --> 00:04:33,678
On the local backend, a Python error
at line 70 of setup_containers.py:

74
00:04:33,678 --> 00:04:36,212
'NoneType' object is not subscriptable.

75
00:04:36,532 --> 00:04:40,478
The program asked the images stack
for its tags, and got nothing.

76
00:04:40,717 --> 00:04:44,159
You see this
if the images stack never finished pulumi up

77
00:04:44,159 --> 00:04:45,730
in this same state bucket.

78
00:04:46,050 --> 00:04:48,909
Skipping it is safe
only when it already ran there.

79
00:04:49,150 --> 00:04:53,514
So we gave the program a stand-in images
stack with placeholder tags;

80
00:04:53,514 --> 00:04:54,590
nothing is built.

81
00:04:54,910 --> 00:04:56,846
Now the real plan appears:

82
00:04:56,846 --> 00:05:01,670
the network, the cluster,
the node pool, the Kubernetes objects,

83
00:05:01,670 --> 00:05:03,102
and + 23 to create.

84
00:05:03,350 --> 00:05:06,851
Two more lines are lookups,
not counted in the 23:

85
00:05:06,851 --> 00:05:09,793
the stack reference and the nginx Service.

86
00:05:10,113 --> 00:05:13,290
In the outputs,
only the cluster name is known;

87
00:05:13,290 --> 00:05:16,940
the address and the URL wait
until the cluster exists.

88
00:05:17,184 --> 00:05:19,063
Pause and run your preview:

89
00:05:19,063 --> 00:05:22,474
you should see the same 23,
with real image tags.

90
00:05:23,617 --> 00:05:27,500
Then the big one:
pulumi up --stack dev --refresh -y.

91
00:05:27,820 --> 00:05:31,745
On the local backend the update starts,
and the very first resource,

92
00:05:31,745 --> 00:05:35,670
the network, fails: invalid_grant:
Invalid grant: account not found.

93
00:05:35,917 --> 00:05:38,625
The network is the first thing
that needs Google,

94
00:05:38,625 --> 00:05:40,504
so from here on this is a picture.

95
00:05:40,824 --> 00:05:44,024
Pause and start yours;
from now on, the meter runs.

96
00:05:46,267 --> 00:05:48,400
Pulumi follows the dependencies.

97
00:05:48,720 --> 00:05:50,662
First the network and its subnet.

98
00:05:50,982 --> 00:05:52,219
Then, side by side,

99
00:05:52,219 --> 00:05:55,409
the router with its NAT gateway,
and the cluster,

100
00:05:55,409 --> 00:05:59,323
the long wait: a quarter of an hour or more,
by our estimate.

101
00:05:59,867 --> 00:06:01,147
Then the node pool.

102
00:06:01,467 --> 00:06:04,952
A Kubernetes provider,
built from the cluster outputs,

103
00:06:04,952 --> 00:06:09,147
waits for that pool,
so no Pod is created before there are nodes.

104
00:06:09,384 --> 00:06:13,220
Through it come the namespace,
the service account of the API

105
00:06:13,220 --> 00:06:17,938
and its binding, the two disk claims,
the frontend and the vector database.

106
00:06:18,184 --> 00:06:21,256
Next, the loader Job
fills the vector database.

107
00:06:21,576 --> 00:06:26,923
Pulumi creates the API
only after the Job completes:

108
00:06:26,923 --> 00:06:30,728
lines 319 to 321 set that dependency.

109
00:06:30,967 --> 00:06:34,743
In parallel,
Helm installs the nginx Ingress controller.

110
00:06:35,063 --> 00:06:37,477
Its Service asks for a load balancer,

111
00:06:37,477 --> 00:06:40,610
so the controllers ask the cloud
for an address:

112
00:06:40,610 --> 00:06:42,764
the part 1 lamp, green this time.

113
00:06:43,084 --> 00:06:46,604
That address becomes a host name,
and the Ingress comes last.

114
00:06:47,584 --> 00:06:49,376
Identity, in two parts.

115
00:06:49,696 --> 00:06:54,669
Pulumi and kubectl reach the API server
through the GKE auth plugin,

116
00:06:54,669 --> 00:06:58,912
fix two from the toolbox,
so there is no cluster password.

117
00:06:59,650 --> 00:07:03,762
And the API Pod wears
its Kubernetes service account,

118
00:07:03,762 --> 00:07:09,271
which Workload Identity swaps
for gcp-service at the door to Vertex AI.

119
00:07:09,591 --> 00:07:11,874
No JSON key sits in the cluster.

120
00:07:12,617 --> 00:07:15,687
Keep in mind: Pulumi connects kubectl once,

121
00:07:15,687 --> 00:07:19,401
inside this toolbox,
which is removed when you exit.

122
00:07:19,721 --> 00:07:21,748
The next session starts unconnected.

123
00:07:22,068 --> 00:07:25,487
If yours stops,
the card shows the four usual causes:

124
00:07:25,487 --> 00:07:27,230
pause and match your error.

125
00:07:37,767 --> 00:07:41,308
Lecture 20 put one nginx container in front.

126
00:07:41,628 --> 00:07:45,148
The game opened its own load balancer,
one door per app.

127
00:07:45,468 --> 00:07:49,372
The cheese app opens one door,
with a receptionist behind it.

128
00:07:49,917 --> 00:07:52,131
The README draws the path:

129
00:07:52,131 --> 00:07:56,646
browser, load balancer,
the nginx Ingress controller,

130
00:07:56,646 --> 00:08:00,481
then the frontend
on 3000 or the API on 9000,

131
00:08:00,481 --> 00:08:03,208
and the vector database on 8000.

132
00:08:03,450 --> 00:08:06,341
A small slip:
the README draws the dotted arrow

133
00:08:06,341 --> 00:08:08,741
from the vector database to the loader.

134
00:08:09,061 --> 00:08:12,858
The data flows the other way,
from the Job into the database.

135
00:08:13,100 --> 00:08:14,337
Follow one click.

136
00:08:14,657 --> 00:08:20,460
<IP>.sslip.io points back to that same IP,
with no setup at all.

137
00:08:20,780 --> 00:08:25,815
The packet passes the cloud load balancer
and lands on the nginx controller.

138
00:08:26,450 --> 00:08:31,182
The Ingress rule starts with the host name:
for that host,

139
00:08:31,182 --> 00:08:37,629
/api-service goes to the API on 9000,
every other path to the frontend on 3000.

140
00:08:37,949 --> 00:08:40,524
The controller follows that rule,

141
00:08:40,524 --> 00:08:44,349
like the nginx.conf of lecture 20,
now generated.

142
00:08:44,884 --> 00:08:47,913
This time the prefix is kept:
nothing strips it.

143
00:08:48,233 --> 00:08:53,772
The API mounts itself under its root path,
/api-service,

144
00:08:53,772 --> 00:08:58,025
so /api-service/status answers version 3.0.

145
00:08:58,967 --> 00:09:01,566
The page comes from the frontend;

146
00:09:01,566 --> 00:09:06,135
then your browser calls the API directly,
at /api-service.

147
00:09:06,934 --> 00:09:12,124
Inside the cluster,
the API reaches Chroma by its Service name,

148
00:09:12,124 --> 00:09:14,102
vector-db, on port 8000.

149
00:09:14,900 --> 00:09:19,999
Now open the bare IP:
the same load balancer, without sslip.io.

150
00:09:20,319 --> 00:09:21,343
What answers?

151
00:09:30,484 --> 00:09:34,111
The nginx controller answers 404 Not Found.

152
00:09:34,431 --> 00:09:36,692
The rule matches only that host name,

153
00:09:36,692 --> 00:09:39,871
so the same door
with the wrong name finds no route.

154
00:09:40,191 --> 00:09:42,409
Use the app_url from the outputs.

155
00:09:42,950 --> 00:09:47,348
That ticks load balancing:
the cloud load balancer at the door,

156
00:09:47,348 --> 00:09:48,482
nginx behind it,

157
00:09:48,482 --> 00:09:52,166
and each Service spreading
requests across its Pods.

158
00:09:53,400 --> 00:09:55,811
Next, try some kubectl commands.

159
00:09:56,131 --> 00:09:59,629
With no cluster here,
all five say connection refused.

160
00:09:59,949 --> 00:10:03,401
You will see exactly this
in any new toolbox session:

161
00:10:03,401 --> 00:10:07,373
the kubeconfig Pulumi wrote went away
with the old container.

162
00:10:07,617 --> 00:10:11,161
The fix is the command Pulumi ran,
an extra here:

163
00:10:11,161 --> 00:10:15,574
gcloud container clusters
get-credentials cheese-app-cluster.

164
00:10:15,894 --> 00:10:18,692
The code passes the region
to the --zone flag;

165
00:10:18,692 --> 00:10:21,185
write --region, which says what it means.

166
00:10:21,933 --> 00:10:25,939
Once connected, get all still shows
only the kubernetes Service,

167
00:10:25,939 --> 00:10:28,568
because the app sits in its own namespace.

168
00:10:28,888 --> 00:10:33,202
With -n cheese-app-namespace,
yours lists the frontend,

169
00:10:33,202 --> 00:10:39,085
database, API and nginx controller Pods,
and a loader Pod marked Completed.

170
00:10:39,405 --> 00:10:41,432
Get nodes shows one node, or two.

171
00:10:42,167 --> 00:10:44,535
The next README step shells into a Pod.

172
00:10:44,855 --> 00:10:47,711
The Pod name in the README
is the author's own;

173
00:10:47,711 --> 00:10:50,081
use the name your first command prints.

174
00:10:50,717 --> 00:10:53,797
Inside,
you get a shell in the API container,

175
00:10:53,797 --> 00:10:55,645
just as we did in the game.

176
00:10:55,965 --> 00:10:58,504
Print its environment
and look for ROOT_PATH.

177
00:10:58,824 --> 00:11:00,018
Pause and try it.

178
00:11:00,917 --> 00:11:05,760
To view the app, the README shows an outputs
block a little older than the code.

179
00:11:06,080 --> 00:11:10,499
Today the address is called ip_address,
not nginx_ingress_ip,

180
00:11:10,499 --> 00:11:13,759
and the Ingress
is called cheese-app-ingress.

181
00:11:14,500 --> 00:11:16,505
Pause and open your app_url.

182
00:11:16,825 --> 00:11:19,686
A footer that says API version:

183
00:11:19,686 --> 00:11:25,316
3.0 proves the whole path works:
it asks /api-service/status.

184
00:11:25,636 --> 00:11:31,055
Then pick the RAG model in the chat;
a reply proves Vertex AI and Chroma too.

185
00:11:31,300 --> 00:11:34,927
Right after deploying
you may see 502 or 503:

186
00:11:34,927 --> 00:11:37,828
the API is still loading TensorFlow.

187
00:11:38,148 --> 00:11:39,641
Wait, and try again.

188
00:11:40,883 --> 00:11:44,808
The README's debugging block
has 14 kubectl commands.

189
00:11:45,128 --> 00:11:46,647
As typed, they all fail;

190
00:11:46,647 --> 00:11:50,888
most say the namespace,
cheese-app-cluster-namespace, is not found.

191
00:11:51,133 --> 00:11:54,657
Lines with <pod-name>
are shell redirections:

192
00:11:54,657 --> 00:11:58,493
replace the whole placeholder,
brackets included.

193
00:11:59,233 --> 00:12:02,644
Two fixes:
the namespace is cheese-app-namespace,

194
00:12:02,644 --> 00:12:07,446
and the Job has one container,
so drop the -c wait-for-chromadb flag.

195
00:12:07,766 --> 00:12:09,025
Pause and try them.

196
00:12:09,766 --> 00:12:12,907
On a Job that exists,
an extra on the stand-in,

197
00:12:12,907 --> 00:12:15,846
the same commands work: done, one succeeded.

198
00:12:16,166 --> 00:12:18,556
Nothing replaces a finished Job's Pod.

199
00:12:19,300 --> 00:12:23,416
A small note:
the disk is mounted at /chroma/chroma,

200
00:12:23,416 --> 00:12:26,660
but today's Chroma image writes to /data.

201
00:12:26,980 --> 00:12:31,439
So the vectors live inside the container
and vanish when the Pod restarts.

202
00:12:31,759 --> 00:12:37,902
Change line 150 to /data,
or run the loader Job again after a restart.

203
00:12:41,050 --> 00:12:42,117
Now scaling.

204
00:12:42,437 --> 00:12:47,287
The lecture promises scaling
on CPU, memory or custom metrics,

205
00:12:47,287 --> 00:12:51,354
but the code sets no replicas
and no Pod autoscaler.

206
00:12:51,674 --> 00:12:54,554
It switches on the node layer:
one to two nodes.

207
00:12:55,300 --> 00:12:58,628
The scheduler packs Pods
onto nodes by their requests.

208
00:12:58,948 --> 00:13:02,596
The frontend asks for a quarter CPU
and 2 gigabytes.

209
00:13:02,916 --> 00:13:05,195
Chroma and nginx ask for little,

210
00:13:05,195 --> 00:13:08,825
and the API for nothing,
so nobody can plan for it.

211
00:13:09,466 --> 00:13:13,903
Scale the frontend to 3;
Milestone 5 asks you to show scaling.

212
00:13:14,223 --> 00:13:19,557
A copy that does not fit waits, pending,
until the autoscaler adds a second node.

213
00:13:19,877 --> 00:13:23,674
Ask for many more, and some stay pending:
the pool stops at two.

214
00:13:24,316 --> 00:13:27,687
The API's disk attaches
to one node at a time.

215
00:13:28,007 --> 00:13:33,105
Suppose you scaled the API to 3,
and some copies landed on the second node.

216
00:13:33,425 --> 00:13:36,562
What happens to them,
and can the autoscaler fix it?

217
00:13:47,700 --> 00:13:51,160
They stay stuck creating,
with a Multi-Attach error:

218
00:13:51,160 --> 00:13:53,289
the disk already sits on node 1.

219
00:13:53,609 --> 00:13:56,510
More nodes do not help;
the limit is the disk.

220
00:13:56,750 --> 00:14:02,374
That ticks scaling, in two levels:
nodes automatically, Pods in your hands;

221
00:14:02,374 --> 00:14:06,947
a Pod autoscaler on CPU needs requests,
and the API has none.

222
00:14:07,267 --> 00:14:09,144
Scale back to 1 when you are done.

223
00:14:10,383 --> 00:14:15,311
Last, delete the cluster:
pulumi destroy --stack dev --refresh -y.

224
00:14:15,631 --> 00:14:20,175
Ours has nothing real to delete;
yours deletes what this stack created,

225
00:14:20,175 --> 00:14:23,695
in reverse order,
from the Ingress down to the network.

226
00:14:24,316 --> 00:14:27,169
Then check what may survive, our suggestion:

227
00:14:27,169 --> 00:14:30,929
list clusters,
forwarding rules, disks and firewall rules.

228
00:14:31,249 --> 00:14:35,266
Those are deleted in the background
and can be left behind;

229
00:14:35,266 --> 00:14:36,967
look for disks named pvc.

230
00:14:37,216 --> 00:14:41,120
Registry images stay on purpose:
delete them in the console.

231
00:14:41,440 --> 00:14:44,704
Delete the lecture 20 machine too,
if it still runs.

232
00:14:45,024 --> 00:14:46,155
Pause and check.

233
00:14:46,416 --> 00:14:47,867
Back to the three asks.

234
00:14:48,187 --> 00:14:51,170
Failover: the ReplicaSet brings Pods back,

235
00:14:51,170 --> 00:14:54,437
and node auto repair
replaces broken machines.

236
00:14:54,757 --> 00:14:59,557
Load balancing: the cloud load balancer,
nginx and the Services.

237
00:14:59,877 --> 00:15:04,059
Scaling: the node autoscaler,
and kubectl scale in your hands.

238
00:15:04,300 --> 00:15:06,199
The lecture lists five payoffs.

239
00:15:06,519 --> 00:15:08,609
Self-service: one pulumi up.

240
00:15:08,929 --> 00:15:11,660
Lower cost: Pods packed by their requests.

241
00:15:11,980 --> 00:15:14,881
Automatic adjustment: the node autoscaler.

242
00:15:15,201 --> 00:15:17,185
Smooth running: self-healing.

243
00:15:17,505 --> 00:15:21,260
Simpler development:
the same images as lecture 20.

244
00:15:21,500 --> 00:15:22,609
Test yourself.

245
00:15:22,929 --> 00:15:26,411
Which control plane part decides
where a new Pod runs,

246
00:15:26,411 --> 00:15:28,860
and which one notices that a Pod died?

247
00:15:29,180 --> 00:15:32,249
Why does get all show only
the kubernetes Service,

248
00:15:32,249 --> 00:15:33,660
and what flag fixes it?

249
00:15:33,900 --> 00:15:36,532
How does the web Service know its Pods,

250
00:15:36,532 --> 00:15:39,703
and why does the API
reach Chroma at vector-db?

251
00:15:40,023 --> 00:15:44,162
Trace one click
from sslip.io to the API Pod;

252
00:15:44,162 --> 00:15:47,105
why does the bare IP answer 404?

253
00:15:47,350 --> 00:15:50,874
Why can the frontend scale to 3
but the API cannot,

254
00:15:50,874 --> 00:15:53,153
and what does the autoscaler add?

255
00:15:53,473 --> 00:15:56,651
Answers are in the exercises
and the study guide.

256
00:15:56,900 --> 00:16:00,212
Next, in lecture 23,
the two Pulumi programs,

257
00:16:00,212 --> 00:16:04,921
in version 4 of the app,
run from GitHub Actions on every merge.

